00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:04.150 align:start position:0%
 
there's much to praise about the remake  of demon souls  it's a remarkable technical showpiece  for the playstation 5 a gripping  gameplay experience that oscillates  between exhilarating  nerve-wracking and downright  heartbreaking and a faithful recreation  of the seminal title that birthed the  souls-like sub-genre  but developer bluepoint's greatest  achievement is that it took something  i'm intimately familiar with  and made me feel like i was venturing  into the unknown  [Music]  fundamentally demon souls for ps5 is  what it always has been  firing some small tweaks the design of  the game is identical to from software's  original  the core mechanics are unchanged the  enemies are placed in the same positions  and behave in the same ways  and the devious tricks and traps are  still there ready to catch the  unfamiliar off guard  but what makes it special is how blue  point has applied its own creative  vision to from software's original  to remake the game not just as it was  but as it was meant to be  realizing its full potential the result  is a title that pays homage to from  software's work  but at the same time stands as brilliant  in its own right  every facet of the environment is rich  even the people who occupy the nexus  have more detail which in turn gives  them greater depth  stockpile thomas a four-long figure who  sits in the nook of the nexus and offers  to look after your excess items and  equipment  tells his story in a way that was not  possible before  my wife and daughter phil  wrecked him to the demons this is the  first time  in many hours of playing demon souls  that i've been able to read the  expression on thomas's faces  and the pain is visible as he recounts  his tragic loss  though their lines may be brief the  voices of these characters sound  familiar  and while some have been re-recorded  with the same actors others are new  but it all sounds right and where new or  tweaked writing and vocal performances  appear  they still evoke the intended effect  where do you call home  dear me i shouldn't pry that is true of  every area in the game  each of the five archstones takes you to  locales that are jaw-dropping visually  and distinct atmospherically this is a  game that in numerous ways  serves as a showcase for all of the ps5  signature features  and hearing demon souls is as gratifying  as seeing and playing it  thanks to the 3d audio through  headphones the heavy  threatening breathing of a blue-eyed  knight told me it was nearby  before i could even see it buzzing flies  and the ragged cores of pecking crows  made the sight of a decaying horse  carcass all the more unsightly  and as archer's fired arrows the sounds  of them whizzing by my ears reveal just  how narrowly i escaped  crucially in each location these new  effects  beautiful lighting and gorgeous  modelling never upend from software's  intent  and in fact create a truer  representation of it  advancement in technology and game  design have given blue point tools to do  what from software couldn't  on ps3 back in 2009 the maze of tunnels  in stone fang feels even more  claustrophobic  and as you venture deeper the rippling  steam coming off lava makes the  sweltering hot environments feel  oppressive  the valley of the firemen's wet wooden  scaffolding looks perilous to walk along  and the torches burn bright blue  violently moving as if agitated  by an unseen toxic gas you feel gross  just standing there  bosses are treated with the same kind of  care either true to the original  or change to more closely embody the  spirit of it the vanguard looks less  like one of those rubber monsters in my  pocket toys from the 90s and more like a  disgusting demonic executioner  the tower knight looms above you  striking a presence that is both heroic  and terrifying  the flame lurker fight because of how  good the animation work is  made me feel like i was a matador  trapped in a lava pit with a demonic  flaming bull constantly bearing down on  me  technically demon souls is astounding on  performance mode it displays gorgeous  visuals rendered at 1440p and upscaled  to 4k  at a consistent smooth frame rate  cinematic mode runs at native 4k  but this seems to come at the cost of  performance as i found the frame rate to  be much less consistent  i stuck to performance mode and did so  with the new offset camera view  which i found to frame the game in a  more cinematic way  features such as the ability to make the  ui dynamically fade in and out do a  great deal  to improve immersion there are also  multiple graphic filters available  including the more muted greener palette  of the original ps3 release  although i think blue point's version  with its richness and vibrancy  should be the way you play for the first  time through if it wasn't clear  demon souls on ps5 is one of the best  looking and sounding games i've ever  played  outside of the presentation bluepoint  has employed a deft to touch  as mentioned previously demon souls on  ps5 plays very much the same  mechanically speaking however bluepoint  has clearly spent a great deal of effort  on improving the feedback  so everything is more impactful there's  a weightiness and heft to the game that  has communicated visually and through  audio  everything from movements to attack  evasion and even consuming item has a  physicality to it  there's a real sense of inertia and  momentum as your blade cuts through the  air  and noticeable resistance as it meets  the steel of armor or the flesh of an  enemy  the visual feedback also instills a  greater sense of danger  you don't want to get hit by things  because it looks and sounds like it  hurts  the physicality now supports and  reinforces the deliberate methodical  nature of souls combat  and that's what stripped me of some of  my confidence the difference in the way  it feels  along with actually being a little rusty  has forced me to once again respect  demon souls  because it's as ruthless as it has ever  been and in many cases more ruthless  than the souls games that followed it  new additions such as unique attack and  finisher animations give weapons more of  an identity than they  had in the original and landing repulse  is such a rush that you want to try and  parry every single enemy  [Music]  but by sticking so closely to from  software's framework bluepoint has also  carried forward some of the more  idiosyncratic aspects of demon souls  gameplay experience  although world tendency is explained a  bit better and more readily visible to  the player  that doesn't make the underlying issues  of it any better it's still for all but  the most learned players quite obtuse as  a concept  the idea of a player struggling dying  and the game becoming harder because of  black world tendency as a result is  suspect  but it's still nonetheless intact in the  remake demon subs for ps5 also features  the same finicky multiplayer system that  it had on ps3  and that from software games continue to  have there are specific conditions that  need to be met and items employed to  enable jolly cooperation  but the information around this isn't  surfaced in a clear visible way for  newcomers to understand  it then falls upon the player to figure  it out but i can't fault bluepoint for  leaving it untouched  warts and all the studio is in the  unenviable position of remaking one of  the most beloved games of all time  which has an incredibly passionate and  vocal fan base and what might seem like  a reasonable change to one person  could be an undermining of what makes  the game unique distinct and memorable  to another  quirks aside blue points remake is an  unmitigated success  it is a technical tour de force and a  true showpiece for what the ps5 and the  power of sony's next-gen console can do  but more importantly it's also a  creative marvel coming from a studio  that is clearly showing the world it has  its own voice  bluepoint is taken from software's  original game and expressed it in a  richer fuller way  and in doing so given me something that  i thought was impossible  the opportunity to relive the experience  of falling in love with souls games for  the first time  [Music]  you

